## Load testing (k6)

Unit / integration / black-box tests verify **correctness**. Load tests verify **performance under concurrency** — an orthogonal concern. CamelBee ships ready-to-run k6 scripts under `docs/k6/` so you can stress-test any interface without authoring load scripts from scratch.

### Where scripts live

`docs/k6/{protocol}/{operation}.js` — one script per operation × protocol. Covered protocols depend on which interfaces you generated the project with.

### Running

Use the `/cb-load-test` skill, or directly:
```bash
k6 run docs/k6/rest/createorder.js --vus 10 --duration 30s
```

### Rules of thumb

| Do | Don't |
|---|---|
| Baseline at 1 VU first (single-user latency) before ramping up | Start at 100 VUs — you won't know what "slow" means without a baseline |
| Disable the CamelBee tracer for realistic numbers (`CAMELBEE_TRACER_ENABLED=false`) | Measure with tracing on — you're measuring the tracer, not the route |
| Use `thresholds` in the script to fail CI on regression | Eyeball the output and "look about the same" |
| Run against a real backend (Postgres, Kafka, etc.) | Run against WireMock — you'll hit WireMock's throughput ceiling, not your app's |

See `.claude/skills/cb-load-test/SKILL.md` for the full workflow.

